first few days of the campaign have seen  Teresa make dogged by questions about  taxes and pensions one man who I'm sure  will provide clear and straightforward  answers is the work and Pensions  Secretary Damian Green who joins us now  from central London and I'm looking  forward to this clear and  straightforward answers mr. green  firstly on reports that the  Conservatives are planning to cap gas  and electricity bills I mean I'm getting  a sense of deja vu here it sounds an  awful lot like the kind of thing ed  Miliband was saying at the last election  we do think the energy market isn't  working properly and we do want to  change the way it operates you'll be  seeing all the details in our manifesto  the difference with the Ed Miliband plan  is that Ed Miliband  just said there would be a freeze  imposed by the government and  immediately after that as you remember  oil prices and therefore gas prices fell  around the world so people wouldn't have  had the advantage of the market changes  what we're going to propose is something  different which is much more sensitive  to movements in the market off gem would  be able to cap but in relation to what  the market price is so that what we will  get rid of is big energy companies  exploiting their customers that I think  is what many people think rightly is  unfair about the energy market and  that's that's what we will do the same  time that it sounds like it's quite a  small difference between a cap and a  freeze if you take into consideration at  your colleague Michael Fallon described  Ed Miliband's policy as extremely  dangerous at the time well they would  have been as I say that and dangerous  and and also as it happens the Ed  Miliband proposal would have cost people  money because the market price then  unexpectedly fell labour would have  imposed a freeze that would have been a  freeze at a higher level then the market  was giving we're proposing as I say a  more flexible system which will be able  to react to the market but which will  mean that any energy company that starts  trying to exploit its customers won't be  able to do do so using off gem the the  regulator to make sure that energy  prices  mein fair okay well it's pretty clear  that you're planning to do something on  energy bills so I wonder if you can be  quite as forthcoming about whether or  not you're going to keep the triple lock  on pensions that guarantees that they'll  go up every year are you gonna keep that  yes or no once we'll be fine the auto  afraid is you'll have to wait for the  manifesto for the details of all our  policies you wouldn't expect me and and  your viewers wouldn't expect me to  partially leak our manifesto now and I'm  afraid I'm not going to do so but but  I'm quite happy for people to inspect  our record on pensions and how we've  protected pensioners thirty forty years  ago forty percent of pensioners in this  country lived in poverty that number is  now down to fourteen percent it's a huge  achievement and of course there's always  more to do but making sure we strike the  right balance for all generations is  clearly very important but the reduction  in pension poverty is something of which  I'm very proud you say that we wouldn't  expect you to spell out your manifesto  of course that's true but at the same  time the Conservatives have acted  quickly to dampen down speculation in  other areas such as Theresa now saying  that the international aid pledge will  stay for example shall we conclude then  that the triple lock on pensions is on  the table  no you should conclude I'm sorry to have  to ask you to be patient Sophie but you  will have to be patient until you have  the manifesto what I can assure you is  that the manifesto will reflect Tereza  mayor's plan for Britain so in the four  big areas where we want to be a global  country we want a stronger economy we  want a fairer society and we want to  keep the united kingdom united that will  run through all the policies in the  manifesto but but you will have to wait  for the detailed policies I'm afraid  okay I'm going to resist from asking the  same question I hope three times and  hoping for an answer how about taxes  will you keep the promise you made last  time that national insurance income tax  and also v80 will not go up under a  Conservative government or a day on the  table this time what well as I say I'll  keep saying I'm afraid of you if you ask  me to give detailed answers about the  manifesto I won't do so but again look  at our record the Conservative Party  believes in low taxes is always a lower  tax party than the alternative  government that the Labour government  and you've seen that that we've taken  four million people out of income tax  altogether over the past few years we've  cut income tax for 31 million people so  you can be absolutely guaranteed that  taxes will always be lower under a  Conservative government than they would  be under a Jeremy Corbyn government so  let me get this straight then you're  talking about a cap on energy bills  you're protecting foreign aid you're not  ruling out tax rises and you're also not  ruling out getting rid of the triple  lock on pensions have you got mixed up  here and got Labour's manifesto in your  hand rather than the Conservatives no I  I'm not telling you all to the  conservative manifesto because I'm  afraid we want to release it at the at  the right time but but you as I say you  can be guaranteed you don't need to look  into a crystal ball or try and you know  find out what might be in the manifesto  you can look at our record it's a record  of even as a time of austerity even when  the public finances  were under extreme pressure we've  managed to find ways to cut taxes for  hard-working people particularly at the  lower end of the income spectrum we  recognize that there are things like  unfairness in society one of which is in  the energy market and and we will deal  with that that's that's a very  